{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788190826,"nonce":"P7eHFdwvXrRVtD0bs8s3YZsk9lM=","message":"RESOLVED on `spec/stable-pawn-order` (4 commits, `-c Release` clean, 0/0). Not\nlaunched — the orchestrator runs `accept/1eb2262-stable-pawn-order.md`.\n\n**Option (1), and it had to be a sort we add rather than an order we document.**\nThe investigation the issue asked for, against the 1.6 decompile:\n\n* `MapPawns.AllPawnsSpawned` — what `pawns` actually walks — IS the raw\n  `pawnsSpawned` `List\u003cPawn\u003e`. `RegisterPawn` appends, `DeRegisterPawn` removes,\n  `UpdateRegistryForPawn` does both, so a faction or host-faction change moves a\n  pawn to the END of the list while it stands still, and a load re-registers in\n  save-file order. No promise of any kind.\n* `RegisterPawn` DOES `InsertionSort` `pawnsInFactionSpawned[Faction.OfPlayer]`\n  by `playerSettings.joinTick` — the closest thing the game has to a stable\n  roster order. Unusable: joinTick ties on every pawn that joined the same tick\n  (`Pawn_PlayerSettings` sets a flat `joinTick = 0`), and it is a different list\n  from the one the verb walks.\n* `Pawn_PlayerSettings.displayOrder` — the order the colonist bar and the\n  Assign/Work tables actually draw in, via `PlayerPawnsDisplayOrderUtility` —\n  defaults to the sentinel `-9999999` and is assigned **lazily by the UI**:\n  `ColonistBar.CheckRecacheEntries` writes that scribed field on any pawn still\n  holding the sentinel. On a bench where the bar has not recached it is the same\n  number for every colonist, and sorting by it would mean depending on a UI\n  side effect on scribed state. **That is a new Class-A write-on-read accessor**\n  and it is recorded in DESIGN.md alongside the decision.\n\n**Correction to the issue's premise.** The spawn was not the cause. `pawns` was\nalready deterministically sorted — attention descending, id tie-break — so\ninserting one pawn cannot permute the others. What moved them is that\n`PawnSerializer.Attention` sums `100 - mood_pct`: two colonists one mood point\napart trade places on any tick that moves either mood, spawn or no spawn. The\nobservation (\"spawning a pawn reordered the existing entries\") is real; the\nattribution is not. The fix is the same either way, but a fix aimed only at\n\"spawning must append\" would have left the actual instability in place.\n\n**What shipped.** Selection and presentation are separated rather than traded\noff: the cap still keeps the most attention-worthy entries (2.6's rule is\nuntouched — the re-sort runs on the survivors only, never on the candidate set),\nand they are emitted by `thingIDNumber` ascending. Both facts are published:\n`order` is `\"id-asc\"` (default) or `\"attention-desc\"`, `selected_by` is always\n`\"attention-desc\"`, a new `order:\"id\"|\"attention\"` arg switches the first, an\nunknown value is a bad-args, and every line carries `attention_rank` so the\nurgency the id order no longer encodes travels with the data. `thingIDNumber` is\nalso what `PawnActs.PawnList` has always sorted `pawns:\"colonists\"` by — the\nACTION side was stable and the OBSERVER side was not, so an agent could act on a\nlist it could not re-read. That inconsistency is now closed.\n\n`digest`'s colonist section deliberately did NOT change: it publishes no `id` at\nall, so it is a glance and not an addressable roster.\n\n**Both halves of the acceptance's third bullet are done.** 3.4's phase 0 now\nselects **A** by predicate — `pawn {sections:[\"work\"]}`, first colonist whose\n`work.disabled` holds neither `Hauling` nor `Doctor` — and **B** as the first\ncolonist that is not **A**, which satisfies 4.9's \"a doctor who is not the\npatient\" by construction. Both drivers (.py and .ps1) and the .md fixture table\nchanged together. The old advice \"make EVERY colonist suitable\" is retired: a\nstable order makes an index reproducible, it does not make it meaningful, and\nthose were two different problems wearing one costume.\n\n**Follow-up, not fixed here (out of scope, flagging rather than filing without\nthe orchestrator's call):** `ThingVerbs` emits an addressable `things` list —\nentries carry `thingIDNumber` — ordered `attention-desc` on a live score\n(forbidden, hp, deterioration). Same shape as this bug. The general rule is\nalready written down in DESIGN.md so the follow-up has a home.","files":null}]}